WEBVTT

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:05.926
Mia, I have shuffled the element
cards: hydrogen, gold, oxygen, sodium.

00:00:06.465 --> 00:00:10.699
Shall we arrange them alphabetically, by
color, or by how expensive they sound?

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:13.898
Gold gets the front row luxury seat.

00:00:14.407 --> 00:00:16.155
Oxygen can sit near the window.

00:00:16.989 --> 00:00:20.201
An entertaining seating plan,
but not the periodic table.

00:00:20.526 --> 00:00:23.830
An element contains atoms with
the same number of protons.

00:00:24.145 --> 00:00:28.328
Ordinary chemical reactions cannot break
an element into simpler substances.

00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:30.511
So oxygen and gold

00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:30.570
are

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:30.917
different

00:00:30.932 --> 00:00:33.757
elements because their atoms
have different proton numbers?

00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:35.260
Exactly.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:40.000
The modern table orders elements by atomic
number, meaning the number of protons.

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:43.835
Hydrogen has one, helium
two, and lithium three.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.545
The layout also groups elements
with similar chemical behavior.

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:51.485
Atomic number, not price
or alphabetical order.

00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:52.241
That would

00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:52.345
have

00:00:52.364 --> 00:00:53.385
cost my imaginary

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:53.842
mark.

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:57.968
Potentially, if the question
asks how the table is arranged.

00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:03.711
Each box usually gives a symbol, name,
atomic number, and relative atomic mass.

00:01:04.122 --> 00:01:07.695
Check the key, because different tables
arrange their labels differently.

00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:13.003
Is relative atomic mass just the number
of protons and neutrons in every atom?

00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:14.426
Careful.

00:01:15.586 --> 00:01:19.577
Mass number counts protons plus
neutrons in one particular atom.

00:01:20.166 --> 00:01:24.083
Relative atomic mass on a table
is usually a weighted average that

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.896
reflects the element's isotopes.

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.290
A decimal does not mean an
atom has part of a neutron.

00:01:30.057 --> 00:01:32.938
No half neutron floating around
looking for its other half.

00:01:33.406 --> 00:01:36.661
I check whether the question
gives a mass number or an average.

00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:37.836
Good.

00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:41.838
Most tables currently show one hundred
and eighteen recognized elements.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:45.370
We will focus on the familiar
ones and the patterns rather

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.189
than memorizing a wall of boxes.

00:01:48.089 --> 00:01:52.316
A map I can reason with sounds better
than a hundred flashcards shouting at me

00:01:52.713 --> 00:01:54.492
Here are three particle tokens.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.033
Protons have positive charge,
neutrons have no charge, and

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:00.832
electrons have negative charge.

00:02:01.295 --> 00:02:04.756
Protons and neutrons occupy
the tiny central nucleus.

00:02:05.106 --> 00:02:07.364
Electrons occupy regions around it.

00:02:08.135 --> 00:02:10.601
Our drawing has electrons on neat circles.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.811
Are those miniature railway tracks?

00:02:13.531 --> 00:02:13.903
No.

00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:18.199
Shell diagrams are useful models
of electron arrangements, not

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.553
photographs of fixed circular paths.

00:02:21.051 --> 00:02:23.954
The nucleus is much smaller
relative to the atom than this

00:02:23.954 --> 00:02:25.739
enlarged diagram suggests.

00:02:26.532 --> 00:02:29.159
Which particle is the
element's identity badge?

00:02:29.766 --> 00:02:30.964
I choose the proton.

00:02:31.705 --> 00:02:32.257
Correct.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.479
Change the proton number
and you change the element.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.636
In a neutral atom, the number of electrons
equals the number of protons, so the

00:02:40.636 --> 00:02:42.809
positive and negative charges balance.

00:02:43.572 --> 00:02:45.417
Sodium has atomic number 11.

00:02:45.746 --> 00:02:48.258
This sodium atom has mass number 23.

00:02:48.663 --> 00:02:52.396
How many protons, neutrons, and
electrons does the neutral atom have?

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:57.263
11 protons, 23 neutrons, and 11 electrons.

00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:00.539
Wait, I have probably
overfilled the nucleus.

00:03:01.307 --> 00:03:01.975
You have.

00:03:02.494 --> 00:03:05.842
23 already includes both
protons and neutrons.

00:03:06.112 --> 00:03:08.301
Subtract 11 to get 12 neutrons.

00:03:08.577 --> 00:03:11.827
11 protons, 12 neutrons, and 11 electrons.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.081
My marking pen can put
away its emergency tissues.

00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:20.293
Mass number is the total in the nucleus,
not the number of neutrons alone.

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:24.128
Now compare carbon 12 and carbon 14.

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:26.133
Both have six protons.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:28.759
One has six neutrons and the other eight.

00:03:29.187 --> 00:03:33.740
They are isotopes, atoms of the same
element with different neutron numbers.

00:03:34.508 --> 00:03:37.472
Same periodic table seat,
different mass number.

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:39.544
They are not different elements.

00:03:40.323 --> 00:03:41.109
Exactly.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.135
Neutral isotopes have the same
electron arrangement, so their chemical

00:03:45.135 --> 00:03:47.277
behavior is very similar at this level.

00:03:47.738 --> 00:03:50.190
Their masses and nuclear
properties can differ.

00:03:50.682 --> 00:03:53.226
We need not call all their
properties identical.

00:03:53.925 --> 00:03:57.099
And losing an electron would not
change the number of protons either?

00:03:57.835 --> 00:03:58.337
Right.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:02.998
Losing or gaining electrons
forms an ion, a charged particle.

00:04:03.274 --> 00:04:06.155
It changes charge, not
the element's identity.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.876
Keep electron changes separate
from changes in the nucleus

00:04:10.293 --> 00:04:14.939
Let us place sodium's 11 electrons
into the simple shell model: two

00:04:14.941 --> 00:04:18.352
in the first shell, eight in the
second, and one in the third.

00:04:18.748 --> 00:04:21.554
The arrangement is two, eight, one.

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:25.327
Does every shell everywhere
stop at eight forever?

00:04:26.058 --> 00:04:26.430
No.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.009
For our introductory examples
among the first 20 elements,

00:04:30.227 --> 00:04:31.668
the simple pattern works.

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:34.349
More advanced arrangements
are more complicated.

00:04:34.703 --> 00:04:37.232
We should not turn a useful
classroom model into a

00:04:37.232 --> 00:04:39.181
universal shell capacity rule.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:40.080
Which direction is a group, a column
going down or a row going across?
